1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,261
Your CTO asks: "Half of the commits a
contractor pushed to the company repository

2
00:00:09,301 --> 00:00:13,885
carry her private email address. She says
she set the work address months ago.

3
00:00:13,925 --> 00:00:14,714
Who is right?"

4
00:00:15,054 --> 00:00:20,686
Both. She did set the work address, in a
separate file, with a rule that says: use

5
00:00:20,726 --> 00:00:26,150
this file for repositories under ~/work.
So where does the private address come

6
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:28,225
from? Say your guess out loud.

7
00:00:33,065 --> 00:00:37,549
The rule has no trailing slash. So it
never matched, and Git said nothing.

8
00:00:37,589 --> 00:00:42,561
Configuration fails silently: a rule that
does not apply produces no warning, only

9
00:00:42,601 --> 00:00:47,512
commits with the wrong address. This video
is about configuration that depends on

10
00:00:47,552 --> 00:00:52,647
where you are, about settings you should
choose on purpose, and about the tools that

11
00:00:52,687 --> 00:00:56,497
make Git say what it is doing.
Keep that missing slash in mind.

12
00:00:56,537 --> 00:00:57,353
It comes back.

13
00:00:59,193 --> 00:01:02,798
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:02,838 --> 00:01:07,638
Configuration is the set of settings that
tell Git how to behave on your machine.

15
00:01:07,678 --> 00:01:12,180
In the last video you learned where
settings come from and which value wins.

16
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:13,136
Now we use that.

17
00:01:13,476 --> 00:01:18,639
Four topics. Include files and conditional
includes: two identities on one machine,

18
00:01:18,679 --> 00:01:23,842
and the ways the rule silently does not
apply. The settings to decide deliberately:

19
00:01:23,882 --> 00:01:28,982
each one has a cost, and "I never set it"
is also a decision, made by the default.

20
00:01:29,022 --> 00:01:34,060
Aliases, your own short names for commands:
what they expand to, and their traps.

21
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:39,326
And environment variables for diagnosis. At
the end I'll show the course's reference

22
00:01:39,366 --> 00:01:42,773
configuration, in which every
line has a stated reason.

23
00:01:44,613 --> 00:01:46,192
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:46,532 --> 00:01:47,944
After this video you can:

25
00:01:48,284 --> 00:01:52,953
Configure two identities with includeIf,
and prove which one a repository uses.

26
00:01:53,293 --> 00:01:56,751
Give the reasons why a conditional
include does not take effect.

27
00:01:57,091 --> 00:02:02,771
Justify a pull, push, fetch, merge, rebase,
diff and rerere setting by its downside.

28
00:02:03,112 --> 00:02:07,656
Write an alias, including a shell alias
with arguments, and trace what it runs.

29
00:02:07,996 --> 00:02:10,535
Trace a Git command with
environment variables.

30
00:02:12,375 --> 00:02:17,602
Includes. In one sentence: include.path
reads another file at the point where the

31
00:02:17,642 --> 00:02:22,999
line stands, and includeIf.<condition>.path
does so only when a condition about the

32
00:02:23,039 --> 00:02:24,754
current repository is true.

33
00:02:25,094 --> 00:02:30,460
The conditions in Git 2.55 are gitdir:
and gitdir/i:, where the location of the

34
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:35,181
repository's .git directory matches
a glob, a pattern with wildcards.

35
00:02:35,221 --> 00:02:40,791
Then onbranch:, the branch that is checked
out. And hasconfig:remote.*.url:, where

36
00:02:40,831 --> 00:02:46,470
some remote of the repository has a matching
URL. In a gitdir pattern, a leading ~/

37
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:51,260
is your home directory, and a trailing
slash means "everything below".

38
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:56,031
Five ways the rule silently does not
apply. The curriculum's objective and the

39
00:02:56,071 --> 00:03:00,616
interview question count four. The textbook
lists five, and I give you all five.

40
00:03:00,656 --> 00:03:04,285
Each one produces commits with
the wrong address and no message.

41
00:03:04,625 --> 00:03:10,745
One, the trailing slash: gitdir:~/work
matches only a repository whose .git

42
00:03:10,785 --> 00:03:12,963
directory is ~/work itself.

43
00:03:13,303 --> 00:03:18,521
Two, order: the include is read where it
stands. If the [user] section comes after

44
00:03:18,561 --> 00:03:22,753
it in the including file, the
general value is read last and wins.

45
00:03:22,793 --> 00:03:25,895
Keep conditional includes
at the end of the file.

46
00:03:26,235 --> 00:03:30,312
Three, not in a repository:
gitdir needs a .git directory.

47
00:03:30,352 --> 00:03:32,512
In ~/work itself there is none.

48
00:03:32,852 --> 00:03:37,463
Four, a missing file: an include that names
a file that does not exist is skipped

49
00:03:37,503 --> 00:03:38,439
without an error.

50
00:03:38,779 --> 00:03:44,562
Five, asking the wrong question: a scope
option such as --global switches include

51
00:03:44,602 --> 00:03:49,666
processing off, so the usual diagnostic
lies unless you add --includes.

52
00:03:50,006 --> 00:03:55,042
A team file, opted into. Git never reads
configuration from the files it tracks.

53
00:03:55,082 --> 00:03:59,927
If it did, cloning a repository would
let its author set your pager, your SSH

54
00:03:59,967 --> 00:04:05,257
command and your hooks. So a team that wants
shared settings commits a file and asks

55
00:04:05,297 --> 00:04:10,270
each member to include it once. Review
changes to such a file like code: it can

56
00:04:10,310 --> 00:04:12,934
define an alias that runs a shell command.

57
00:04:13,274 --> 00:04:18,177
The settings to decide deliberately. The
textbook lists twenty settings in a table

58
00:04:18,217 --> 00:04:22,698
with six columns: what it does, why it
matters, its downside, whether it is

59
00:04:22,738 --> 00:04:25,953
optional, and whether it is
personal or a team matter.

60
00:04:25,993 --> 00:04:30,775
Read the downside column aloud for the
seven kinds this video's objective names.

61
00:04:31,115 --> 00:04:35,162
user.name and user.email:
required, one per context.

62
00:04:35,502 --> 00:04:39,523
init.defaultBranch: the name of
the first branch. Recommended.

63
00:04:39,863 --> 00:04:45,202
pull.rebase: how git pull integrates. With
nothing set, a diverged pull is fatal.

64
00:04:45,242 --> 00:04:50,580
Downside of true: it gives unpublished
commits new IDs and flattens local merges.

65
00:04:50,920 --> 00:04:54,865
pull.ff=only: pull refuses
anything but a fast-forward.

66
00:04:54,905 --> 00:05:00,299
In configuration it wins over pull.rebase,
so a diverged pull needs a flag.

67
00:05:00,638 --> 00:05:03,180
push.default: leave the default, simple.

68
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:09,060
push.autoSetupRemote, which the textbook
dates to Git 2.37: the first push of a

69
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:14,711
branch sets its upstream. Downside: a
mistyped branch name is published at once.

70
00:05:15,051 --> 00:05:19,332
fetch.prune: fetch deletes remote-tracking
branches whose branch is gone.

71
00:05:19,372 --> 00:05:23,238
Downside: that ref may have been
your last name for those commits.

72
00:05:23,578 --> 00:05:29,605
merge.conflictStyle, with zdiff3 since
Git 2.35: adds the common ancestor to a

73
00:05:29,645 --> 00:05:34,584
conflict. Downside: longer conflict
regions, and a third marker.

74
00:05:34,924 --> 00:05:40,010
rebase.autoSquash: downside, a
title that starts with fixup!

75
00:05:40,050 --> 00:05:41,804
by accident is moved.

76
00:05:42,144 --> 00:05:45,552
rebase.autoStash: the
final apply can conflict.

77
00:05:45,892 --> 00:05:51,404
rebase.updateRefs, Git 2.38: it
moves branches you did not name.

78
00:05:51,744 --> 00:05:55,819
rerere.enabled: records conflict
resolutions and replays them.

79
00:05:55,859 --> 00:05:58,806
Downside: a wrong
resolution is replayed too.

80
00:05:59,146 --> 00:06:03,795
diff.algorithm: myers is the default, and
histogram reads better when code moves.

81
00:06:03,835 --> 00:06:08,484
Downside: your diff differs from a
colleague's. The setting changes what you see,

82
00:06:08,524 --> 00:06:09,700
never what is stored.

83
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:13,757
diff.colorMoved: colors moved
lines, in the terminal only.

84
00:06:14,097 --> 00:06:16,148
core.editor: recommended.

85
00:06:16,488 --> 00:06:20,005
core.autocrlf: optional,
and unset on macOS.

86
00:06:20,345 --> 00:06:23,371
core.excludesFile: your
personal ignore file.

87
00:06:23,711 --> 00:06:28,700
commit.gpgSign signs every commit, and
fails every commit when the key is

88
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:29,526
unavailable.

89
00:06:29,866 --> 00:06:35,010
And one more with a sharp edge:
help.autocorrect. immediate runs a guess.

90
00:06:35,050 --> 00:06:38,063
Since Git 2.49 the value
1 means immediate.

91
00:06:38,403 --> 00:06:43,424
The textbook's rule for teams: split the
few settings a team must agree on, such as

92
00:06:43,464 --> 00:06:47,997
the default branch name, line-ending
attributes, and whether signatures are

93
00:06:48,037 --> 00:06:51,229
required, from everything
else, which stays personal.

94
00:06:51,269 --> 00:06:53,119
And put a comment on each line.

95
00:06:53,459 --> 00:06:58,458
Aliases. In one sentence: an alias is a
configuration value under alias.<name> that

96
00:06:58,498 --> 00:07:03,497
Git substitutes for the name when it is the
first word of a command line, appending

97
00:07:03,537 --> 00:07:04,832
any further arguments.

98
00:07:05,172 --> 00:07:10,228
If the value starts with !, it's handed
to the shell and run from the top-level

99
00:07:10,268 --> 00:07:14,292
directory of the repository.
Otherwise it's a Git command line.

100
00:07:14,332 --> 00:07:19,710
An alias can't replace an existing command.
Defining one is git config set, which is

101
00:07:19,750 --> 00:07:20,355
🟡 CAUTION.

102
00:07:20,695 --> 00:07:26,335
Two traps, both about arguments. Arguments
after the alias name are appended to the

103
00:07:26,375 --> 00:07:31,605
expansion. And for a shell alias, Git
runs sh -c with the alias and "$@": the

104
00:07:31,645 --> 00:07:35,232
arguments are available as
$1, and are also appended.

105
00:07:35,272 --> 00:07:38,860
The manual's remedy is a
function that consumes them.

106
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:44,592
Never use aliases in scripts, hooks or CI:
they exist in your configuration only.

107
00:07:44,932 --> 00:07:49,076
The textbook carries one caveat here
that I repeat: the manual documents a

108
00:07:49,116 --> 00:07:53,260
subsection form for alias names with
unusual characters, and which release

109
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:55,352
introduced that form was not checked.

110
00:07:55,692 --> 00:08:00,411
Environment variables for diagnosis.
Configuration says what Git should do.

111
00:08:00,451 --> 00:08:03,330
These variables make Git
say what it is doing.

112
00:08:03,670 --> 00:08:09,308
GIT_TRACE=1: alias expansion, and the
built-in and external commands that are

113
00:08:09,348 --> 00:08:14,987
started. GIT_TRACE_SETUP=1: the .git
directory, the working tree, the current

114
00:08:15,027 --> 00:08:21,108
directory and prefix Git settled on. Use
it when Git picks up the wrong repository,

115
00:08:21,148 --> 00:08:26,196
or none. GIT_TRACE_PACKET=1: the
protocol conversation with a remote.

116
00:08:26,236 --> 00:08:32,243
GIT_TRACE_CURL=1: the HTTP exchange.
GIT_SSH_COMMAND: replaces the ssh program and

117
00:08:32,283 --> 00:08:37,700
its options for this command.
GIT_TRACE2_PERF=1: timings inside a command.

118
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:43,674
A value of 1, 2 or true writes to standard
error, and an absolute path appends to

119
00:08:43,714 --> 00:08:44,411
that file.

120
00:08:44,751 --> 00:08:48,802
The HTTP variables need a server,
so they are not run here.

121
00:08:48,842 --> 00:08:53,448
The textbook's version note: older
tutorials name GIT_CURL_VERBOSE.

122
00:08:53,488 --> 00:08:58,440
The Git 2.55 manual documents
GIT_TRACE_CURL. Since which release is not

123
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:03,987
established. Keep GIT_TRACE_REDACT at its
default, so that Authorization headers

124
00:09:04,027 --> 00:09:07,246
stay out of the output
you paste into a ticket.

125
00:09:09,086 --> 00:09:13,917
Two small pictures. For includes, the
textbook's analogy: a footnote that says

126
00:09:13,957 --> 00:09:18,602
"insert appendix D here, but only in
the edition for the Bengaluru office".

127
00:09:18,642 --> 00:09:23,598
The analogy breaks because order matters:
text inserted early is overridden by a

128
00:09:23,638 --> 00:09:25,659
later line of the including file.

129
00:09:25,999 --> 00:09:31,012
For aliases: a speed-dial key. It breaks
at the arguments: whatever you type after

130
00:09:31,052 --> 00:09:33,908
the alias is added to the
end of the expansion.

131
00:09:34,248 --> 00:09:37,892
And one frame for the whole
video: configuration is silent.

132
00:09:37,932 --> 00:09:42,762
A rule that does not match, a key that
is misspelled, an alias that expands to

133
00:09:42,802 --> 00:09:47,382
something else: none of them prints a
warning. So the skill is not writing

134
00:09:47,422 --> 00:09:52,439
configuration. It's asking Git what it
read, with --show-origin, and what it ran,

135
00:09:52,479 --> 00:09:53,376
with GIT_TRACE.

136
00:09:55,216 --> 00:09:59,856
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper.
Write the gitdir condition for every

137
00:09:59,896 --> 00:10:03,840
repository under a folder named
clients in your home directory.

138
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:06,749
Mind the last character.
Then say it out loud.

139
00:10:11,589 --> 00:10:17,295
It's gitdir:~/clients/, with the trailing
slash. You'll meet that exact rule in the

140
00:10:17,335 --> 00:10:19,579
demo. Now the textbook's drawing.

141
00:10:19,919 --> 00:10:25,484
On the left, the personal file: a [user]
section first, and the conditional include

142
00:10:25,524 --> 00:10:31,088
last. On the right, the work file with one
line. The arrow is the include: the work

143
00:10:31,128 --> 00:10:35,140
file is read at that point, and
only if the condition holds.

144
00:10:35,180 --> 00:10:40,812
Under the boxes, the two reading orders.
Inside ~/work, two values are read, and the

145
00:10:40,852 --> 00:10:43,311
later one wins. Elsewhere, one value.

146
00:10:45,151 --> 00:10:48,913
Into the lab. In this demo the
identity comes from configuration.

147
00:10:48,953 --> 00:10:53,827
The lab library normally pins it through
environment variables, which would hide the

148
00:10:53,867 --> 00:10:54,236
effect.

149
00:10:54,576 --> 00:10:57,607
Before: both repositories
use the same address.

150
00:10:57,947 --> 00:11:03,964
A second file with the work address, and
one git config set --global 🟡 CAUTION for

151
00:11:04,004 --> 00:11:10,095
the conditional include. Predict what git
config get --all --show-origin user.email

152
00:11:10,135 --> 00:11:13,862
prints inside the work
repository. Say it out loud.

153
00:11:18,702 --> 00:11:23,227
Two global values, and the second,
from the included file, wins.

154
00:11:23,267 --> 00:11:26,009
In ~/oss/evalkit, the file is not read.

155
00:11:26,349 --> 00:11:29,468
The commits prove it: the new
commit carries the work address.

156
00:11:29,808 --> 00:11:34,446
Now the failures. For each one,
predict the address before the output.

157
00:11:34,486 --> 00:11:38,790
First, the same rule without its
trailing slash. Say it out loud.

158
00:11:38,830 --> 00:11:39,458
I'll wait.

159
00:11:44,298 --> 00:11:48,927
No trailing slash: the personal address.
This is the contractor, and the missing

160
00:11:48,967 --> 00:11:50,269
slash you kept in mind.

161
00:11:50,609 --> 00:11:56,086
Quick quiz. Next come the same two sections
in the other order: the conditional

162
00:11:56,126 --> 00:12:01,742
include first, and the [user] section after
it. Inside the work repository, which

163
00:12:01,782 --> 00:12:05,862
address wins? A, the work
address. B, the personal address.

164
00:12:05,902 --> 00:12:06,700
Your answer?

165
00:12:11,540 --> 00:12:16,012
B. The same two sections in the other
order: the general value is read last, and

166
00:12:16,052 --> 00:12:16,294
wins.

167
00:12:16,634 --> 00:12:21,124
In ~/work itself, which is not a
repository, the rule does not apply.

168
00:12:21,164 --> 00:12:23,618
In a new repository below it, it does.

169
00:12:23,958 --> 00:12:26,113
A missing file is skipped
without an error.

170
00:12:26,453 --> 00:12:32,565
And the diagnostic that lies: git config
get --global user.email prints the personal

171
00:12:32,605 --> 00:12:37,253
address, because naming a scope
switches include processing off.

172
00:12:37,293 --> 00:12:38,351
Add --includes.

173
00:12:38,691 --> 00:12:43,032
By remote, not by directory: the identity
changes the moment the remote is added.

174
00:12:43,372 --> 00:12:47,502
A team file, opted into with one
include.path in .git/config.

175
00:12:47,542 --> 00:12:51,740
A relative path is resolved
against the file that contains it.

176
00:12:54,580 --> 00:12:59,167
git var GIT_DEFAULT_BRANCH: main with
the lab's global file, master with none.

177
00:12:59,507 --> 00:13:03,951
help.autocorrect: immediate ran the
guess. With a typo that resolves to a

178
00:13:03,991 --> 00:13:06,654
destructive command, it
would have run that.

179
00:13:14,994 --> 00:13:17,935
A function was moved to the
top of a file and edited.

180
00:13:17,975 --> 00:13:22,602
Both diffs are correct. histogram reports
one block added and one removed, which is

181
00:13:22,642 --> 00:13:23,389
what happened.

182
00:13:29,229 --> 00:13:31,349
Five aliases that earn their place.

183
00:13:31,689 --> 00:13:37,126
st is status -sb. lg draws every ref as a
graph: the fourth command of the diagnosis

184
00:13:37,166 --> 00:13:42,278
ritual, in two letters. last shows the
newest commit with the files it touched.

185
00:13:42,618 --> 00:13:47,694
unstage is restore --staged --, 🟡
CAUTION, as video 17 explained.

186
00:13:47,734 --> 00:13:51,551
The trailing -- makes
everything after it a path.

187
00:13:51,891 --> 00:13:58,145
amend 🟡 CAUTION is commit --amend --no-edit.
Everything video 21 said about amending

188
00:13:58,185 --> 00:14:00,093
published commits applies.

189
00:14:00,433 --> 00:14:05,250
GIT_TRACE=1 prints the expansion, and
then the built-in command that runs.

190
00:14:05,590 --> 00:14:11,097
Arguments are appended. That's convenient
for lg -2, and a trap for an alias that

191
00:14:11,137 --> 00:14:12,193
hard-codes HEAD.

192
00:14:12,533 --> 00:14:17,651
A shell alias runs in the top-level
directory, although you were in gateway/.

193
00:14:17,691 --> 00:14:20,666
GIT_PREFIX holds the
directory you came from.

194
00:14:21,006 --> 00:14:25,262
Predict what git tracked HEAD does when
the alias uses $1 in the middle of a

195
00:14:25,302 --> 00:14:25,771
pipeline.

196
00:14:30,611 --> 00:14:34,999
The argument is available as $1,
and is also appended, here to head.

197
00:14:35,039 --> 00:14:39,949
The trace shows the command line Git
started. The function form consumes the

198
00:14:39,989 --> 00:14:40,600
arguments.

199
00:14:40,940 --> 00:14:43,902
alias.status is ignored:
built-in commands win.

200
00:14:56,742 --> 00:15:01,334
When Git "does not see" a repository,
or sees a parent directory's repository

201
00:15:01,374 --> 00:15:03,741
instead of yours, these lines say which.

202
00:15:08,281 --> 00:15:13,634
GIT_SSH_COMMAND, shown without a network:
a stand-in script named ssh prints its

203
00:15:13,674 --> 00:15:19,297
arguments and exits. No connection is made.
Git appended its own arguments to yours.

204
00:15:28,137 --> 00:15:33,355
The course's reference configuration. Read
the comments: every line has a reason.

205
00:15:33,395 --> 00:15:38,418
Notice where the conditional include
stands: last. And notice the alias pushf.

206
00:15:38,458 --> 00:15:43,871
The textbook calls it the alias most worth
having, because the safe form of a forced

207
00:15:43,911 --> 00:15:47,312
push is long enough that
people type --force instead.

208
00:15:47,352 --> 00:15:51,337
Part 2 explains that command.
Please don't use it before then.

209
00:15:53,177 --> 00:15:54,825
Five mistakes to watch for.

210
00:15:55,165 --> 00:16:00,557
An includeIf rule that never matches. Root
cause: the gitdir pattern has no trailing

211
00:16:00,597 --> 00:16:04,696
slash, so it matches only a .git
directory at exactly that path.

212
00:16:05,036 --> 00:16:10,220
The rule matches and the wrong value still
wins. Root cause: the [user] section

213
00:16:10,260 --> 00:16:14,651
stands after the include in the
file, and the value read last wins.

214
00:16:14,991 --> 00:16:19,413
git config get --global user.email
shows the "wrong" address.

215
00:16:19,453 --> 00:16:25,410
Root cause: naming a scope switches include
processing off; add --includes, or ask

216
00:16:25,450 --> 00:16:28,189
without a scope inside the repository.

217
00:16:28,529 --> 00:16:33,595
A shell alias receives its argument twice.
Root cause: Git runs the alias with "$@"

218
00:16:33,635 --> 00:16:37,656
appended; wrap the body in a function
that consumes the arguments.

219
00:16:37,996 --> 00:16:43,411
A build script uses an alias and fails
on CI. Root cause: aliases exist in your

220
00:16:43,451 --> 00:16:44,722
configuration only.

221
00:16:46,562 --> 00:16:52,129
Now, out of the lab, and back to the
contractor, as the textbook resolves it.

222
00:16:52,169 --> 00:16:57,372
She did set her work address, in
~/.gitconfig-work, with an includeIf on

223
00:16:57,412 --> 00:17:02,469
gitdir:~/work. The pattern has no
trailing slash, so it never matched.

224
00:17:02,509 --> 00:17:08,440
Diagnose with git config get --all
--show-origin user.email inside the repository.

225
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:14,193
Fix the pattern. Then set user.useConfigOnly=true
and remove the global default

226
00:17:14,233 --> 00:17:19,508
address, so that a repository outside
both directories refuses to commit.

227
00:17:19,548 --> 00:17:23,076
And published commits keep
the address they have.

228
00:17:23,416 --> 00:17:28,954
A second one from the textbook. A platform
team runs a script of git config set

229
00:17:28,994 --> 00:17:34,884
--global lines on every laptop. Six months
later nobody remembers why pull.rebase is

230
00:17:34,924 --> 00:17:40,673
true. A comment on each line, and a split
between team settings and personal ones,

231
00:17:40,713 --> 00:17:46,110
prevent that. And the first reply to
"Git does something strange on the build

232
00:17:46,150 --> 00:17:51,969
machine" is two commands run there: git
config list --show-scope --show-origin, and

233
00:17:52,009 --> 00:17:54,582
the failing command with GIT_TRACE=1.

234
00:17:56,422 --> 00:18:00,619
Your turn. Do Lab 5.2, "Two
identities with includeIf", in

235
00:18:00,659 --> 00:18:04,857
lab-manual/m05-configuration.md.
The lab is a sealed room.

236
00:18:04,897 --> 00:18:10,556
Its first step points the global
configuration at a per-lab file inside it, so

237
00:18:10,596 --> 00:18:14,428
nothing the lab sets reaches
your real configuration.

238
00:18:14,468 --> 00:18:15,962
Please don't skip it.

239
00:18:16,302 --> 00:18:21,491
Before each git config get --show-origin
user.email, predict the address and the

240
00:18:21,531 --> 00:18:26,784
file it comes from. In the failure scenario,
predict which of the five causes you

241
00:18:26,824 --> 00:18:28,091
are about to create.

242
00:18:29,931 --> 00:18:35,479
Q57: "Your includeIf rule for ~/work/
exists, yet a work repository commits with

243
00:18:35,519 --> 00:18:41,137
your personal address. Give four causes
and the command that distinguishes them."

244
00:18:43,477 --> 00:18:48,146
Answer out loud. A strong answer names
causes that are different in kind: about the

245
00:18:48,186 --> 00:18:52,740
pattern, about position in the file, about
the file that is included, about where

246
00:18:52,780 --> 00:18:57,108
the repository is, about how you asked.
For each, say what the distinguishing

247
00:18:57,148 --> 00:19:01,136
command would print. You know five.
Give the four you can explain best.

248
00:19:02,976 --> 00:19:08,240
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say: includeIf reads another file when a

249
00:19:08,280 --> 00:19:13,480
condition about the repository holds, and
I keep it last in my global file so that

250
00:19:13,520 --> 00:19:18,784
it wins. It fails silently when the gitdir
pattern lacks its trailing slash, when a

251
00:19:18,824 --> 00:19:24,088
later line overrides it, outside a repository,
when the file is missing, and when I

252
00:19:24,128 --> 00:19:26,772
ask with a scope option and no --includes.

253
00:19:27,112 --> 00:19:30,753
Every setting I choose has
a downside that I can name.

254
00:19:30,793 --> 00:19:36,206
An alias is substituted by Git and gets
my arguments appended, and a shell alias

255
00:19:36,246 --> 00:19:41,522
runs from the top of the repository. And
GIT_TRACE=1 shows what Git ran, while

256
00:19:41,562 --> 00:19:44,930
GIT_TRACE_SETUP=1 shows
which repository it found.

257
00:19:46,770 --> 00:19:51,768
Read sections 14B.4 to
14B.7 of Chapter 14B.

258
00:19:52,108 --> 00:19:58,433
Do Lab 5.3, "Aliases", in
lab-manual/m05-configuration.md, then fill in the

259
00:19:58,473 --> 00:20:05,307
worksheet of Lab 5.4, "Your deliberate
configuration (a worksheet)", and keep it;

260
00:20:05,347 --> 00:20:06,750
V174 revisits it.

261
